WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.436
Hi, I'm Bramblewood!

00:00:02.436 --> 00:00:07.786
Halo trains mixture-of-experts models up to two point eight times faster than stock TRL.

00:00:07.786 --> 00:00:09.697
Here's where that comes from.

00:00:11.142 --> 00:00:15.232
The gap is mostly structural, not kernels: Halo sends each token to

00:00:15.232 --> 00:00:20.003
the one GPU that owns its expert, instead of summing the full output everywhere.

00:00:21.692 --> 00:00:25.678
In stock transformers, every GPU keeps the whole batch, zeroes the experts

00:00:25.678 --> 00:00:29.664
it doesn't own, and sums the full output across all of them.

00:00:30.084 --> 00:00:31.419
Halo moves tokens instead.

00:00:31.419 --> 00:00:35.423
Each one travels once to the GPU holding its expert, and comes

00:00:35.423 --> 00:00:37.091
back to where it started.

00:00:38.842 --> 00:00:41.411
Each GPU starts with its own tokens.

00:00:41.411 --> 00:00:45.081
A router, in full precision, picks each token's top experts.

00:00:45.501 --> 00:00:50.014
Dispatch sends every token once to the GPU that owns its expert.

00:00:50.014 --> 00:00:53.775
There, one grouped matrix multiply runs all the local experts.

00:00:54.195 --> 00:00:57.463
Combine sends each result home, same rows, same order.

00:00:57.463 --> 00:01:00.369
Every GPU still trains on its own batch.

00:01:00.789 --> 00:01:05.106
So the wire carries each token's share, not a full-tensor sum on every GPU.

00:01:06.792 --> 00:01:11.567
Stock TRL shards every weight eight ways and re-gathers all twenty point

00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:13.557
seven billion parameters every micro-step.

00:01:13.977 --> 00:01:15.688
Halo's default keeps them resident.

00:01:15.688 --> 00:01:20.136
More memory, less waiting, and that difference sits inside the two point eight.

00:01:21.592 --> 00:01:25.017
Sharded the same way on both sides, Halo still leads: one point

00:01:25.017 --> 00:01:29.012
two five times at four K, and two point six eight at sixteen K.

00:01:30.642 --> 00:01:32.062
It isn't the kernel.

00:01:32.062 --> 00:01:36.321
Even on the slow per-expert loop, Halo beats TRL's fast grouped kernel.

00:01:36.321 --> 00:01:38.450
The lead is structural, routing first.

00:01:40.192 --> 00:01:44.541
To recap: route tokens, don't sum outputs, and know which sharding you're comparing.

00:01:44.961 --> 00:01:47.229
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:47.877
I'm Bramblewood.

00:01:47.877 --> 00:01:48.201
Bye!
